    <h1 class="article-title">How to Migrate From SSRS to Power BI Step by Step</h1>
    <p class="article-dek">Migrate SSRS reports to Power BI with a step-by-step RDL checklist. Compare paginated reports, interactive Power BI reports, and Power BI Report Server.</p>

    <div class="article-byline">
      <div class="article-avatar" style="background:#4C9C8B;">NK</div>
      <div><div class="article-author">Nathalie</div><div class="article-role">Consultant, Tycoon Clap</div></div>
      <div class="article-time">4 min read</div>
    </div>

    <div class="article-hero-art"><svg viewBox="0 0 240 160" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="28" y="24" width="184" height="112" rx="14" fill="#DCEFE9"/><rect x="86" y="84" width="14" height="24" fill="#4C9C8B"/><rect x="108" y="68" width="14" height="40" fill="#E9584C"/><rect x="130" y="52" width="14" height="56" fill="#2E3A55"/></svg></div>

    <div class="article-grid">
      <article class="article-content" id="articleContent">
        <div class="qa-box"><span class="qa-label">Quick answer</span><p>Migrate compatible SSRS .rdl files to the Power BI service as paginated reports. Rebuild reports as interactive .pbix files when users need exploration, and evaluate Power BI Report Server when the reporting platform must stay on-premises. Pilot the move before changing production links or subscriptions.</p></div>
        <p class="updated-note">Last updated November 2026</p>
        <p>This guide to Reporting Services and Power BI covers cloud and on-premises migration paths. Use the <a href="how-to-brief-a-business-intelligence-analyst.html">BI project checklist</a> to assign report owners and agree on acceptance tests before you begin.</p>
        <h2 id="destination">SSRS vs Power BI: Choose the destination</h2>
<p>When teams compare Power BI and Reporting Services, the practical question is how people use each report. A monthly invoice batch has different requirements from a sales dashboard with slicers and drill-down. Choose the destination report by report.</p><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Requirement</th><th scope="col">Destination</th><th scope="col">What moves</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fixed-layout PDFs, invoices, and long tables</td><td>Power BI service paginated report</td><td>Compatible RDL definition, with connections and publishing reviewed.</td></tr><tr><td>Interactive trend analysis and cross-filtering</td><td>Power BI interactive report</td><td>Rebuild the experience in Power BI Desktop and validate the measures.</td></tr><tr><td>On-premises hosting required</td><td>Power BI Report Server</td><td>Follow the separate report-server migration path based on source mode and version.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="inventory">1. Inventory SSRS reports and dependencies</h2>
<p>Capture the server version and mode, folder path, report owner, usage, business criticality, .rdl file, parameters and defaults, linked reports, shared data sources and datasets, subreports, subscriptions, embedded URLs, and export formats. Track which reports need month-end runs even if they are rarely opened. Assign a disposition to each report: migrate, rebuild, keep temporarily, or retire with the owner’s approval.</p>
<h2 id="compatibility">2. Assess RDL compatibility</h2>
<p>Check supported data sources and authentication, external images and fonts, custom assemblies, linked reports, and renderer-specific layouts. Microsoft’s migration tooling can embed supported shared data sources and datasets into each RDL. Plan ownership and repeatable updates for those embedded connections. References to custom code DLLs require remediation. Open representative reports in Power BI Report Builder and record compatibility failures before committing to the schedule.</p><p>Use Microsoft’s <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/migrate-ssrs-reports-to-power-bi">RDL migration planning guide</a> to verify limitations for your reports.</p>
<h2 id="prepare">3. Prepare the workspace, gateway, and access</h2>
<p>Set up a pilot workspace and confirm publisher permissions, viewer licensing, and capacity requirements for your tenant. Map SSRS folder and report access to your target workspace, app, and item access. Configure the supported connection and credentials for each report. If private data sources require an on-premises data gateway, set up the gateway connection and validate it from the service before migration. Test access as both an authorized and a restricted viewer.</p>
<h2 id="publish">4. Publish an RDL as a Power BI paginated report</h2>
<ol><li><strong>SSRS 2022.</strong> In the web portal, open the report or folder, choose <strong>Publish</strong>, select the reports, sign in, and pick the pilot workspace. Review the migration summary.</li><li><strong>Earlier SSRS versions.</strong> Use the RDL Migration Tool shipped with Power BI Report Builder. Follow Microsoft’s instructions for dependencies and supported sources, and fix failures before retrying.</li><li><strong>Individual files.</strong> Open the RDL in Power BI Report Builder, update connections and unsupported features as needed, then publish and test the report in the target workspace.</li></ol><p>The resulting report is a paginated .rdl report. For interactive use, build a semantic model and .pbix report, then validate business logic and totals against the original report.</p><p>See Microsoft’s <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/publish-reporting-services-power-bi-service">portal publishing instructions</a> and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/using-rdl-migration-tool">RDL Migration Tool documentation</a>.</p>
<h2 id="validate">5. Validate the output in both environments</h2>
<p>Run both reports against the same frozen data period and parameter values. Check row counts, totals, date boundaries, blank values, multi-select parameters, drill-through links, and performance. For PDF and Excel users, compare page breaks, headers, fonts, exported data, and filenames where downstream automation depends on them. Recreate supported subscriptions as needed, test recipients and schedules, and confirm the result with each report owner.</p>
<h2 id="cutover">6. Cut over in waves and keep a rollback path</h2>
<p>Start with a small group of reports, including one high-use report and one with complex dependencies. For each wave, get owner approval, update links and schedules, and monitor usage, failures, and runtime. Coordinate delivery so recipients get one production subscription during the overlap. Keep the original reporting path available until critical business cycles pass. If figures, access, or scheduled delivery fail, restore the previous links or schedules while you correct the target.</p>
<h2 id="report-server">Power BI Report Server migration for on-premises hosting</h2>
<p>For a native-mode SSRS migration, Microsoft documents backing up the encryption key and report server databases, cloning the report server database to the target SQL Server, installing Power BI Report Server, and configuring the target with the cloned database and restored key. Check source and target version compatibility before proceeding and follow the separate instructions for SharePoint-integrated mode where applicable. Revalidate permissions, report connections, and subscriptions on the target server.</p><p>Follow Microsoft’s <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/report-server/migrate-report-server">report-server installation migration guide</a> for the full sequence.</p>
        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
        <details open><summary>Can I convert SSRS reports to interactive Power BI reports automatically?</summary><p class="faq-a">Microsoft’s RDL migration tools publish compatible SSRS reports as paginated RDL reports. Build a separate interactive report in Power BI Desktop when the use case requires a PBIX report.</p></details>
<details><summary>Do SSRS subscriptions and permissions move with an RDL?</summary><p class="faq-a">Treat subscriptions and access as separate migration tasks. Configure and test the target permissions and supported delivery schedules before cutover.</p></details>
<details><summary>Which Power BI destination works as an on-premises SSRS alternative?</summary><p class="faq-a">Evaluate Power BI Report Server when you need on-premises hosting. Use Microsoft’s report-server migration guidance and check the supported versions and licensing for your setup.</p></details>
        </div>
